arXiv:2510.01098v1 [stat.ML] 01 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Blake Bordelon
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mary Letey
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Cengiz Pehlevan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Center for Mathematical Sciences
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Applications
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">John A. Paulson School of Engineering
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Applied Sciences
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kempner Institute for the Study of Natural
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Artificial Intelligence Harvard University
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study in-context learning (ICL) of linear regression in a deep linear self-attention model, characterizing how performance depends on various computational and statistical resources (width, depth, number of training steps, batch size and data per context). In a joint limit where data dimension, context length, and residual stream width scale proportionally, we analyze the limiting asymptotics for three ICL settings: (1) isotropic covariates and tasks (ISO), (2) fixed and structured covariance (FS), and (3) where covariances are randomly rotated and structured (RRS). For ISO and FS settings, we find that depth only aids ICL performance if context length is limited. Alternatively, in the RRS setting where covariances change across contexts, increasing the depth leads to significant improvements in ICL, even at infinite context length. This provides a new solvable toy model of neural scaling laws which depends on both width and depth of a transformer and predicts an optimal transformer shape as a function of compute. This toy model enables computation of exact asymptotics for the risk as well as derivation of powerlaws under source/capacity conditions for the ICL tasks.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, transformer models have become the backbone architecture for modern machine learning systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. The transformer architecture consists of alternating self attention and multi-layer perceptron blocks in a deep residual network. For a variety of tasks, increasing the size of transformers by increasing width and depth of the model leads to empirically predictable improvements in model performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hestness et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffmann et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Various protocols for scaling up the width and depth of transformers have been developed that provide stable training limits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chizat &amp; Netrapalli, 2024</a>)</cite>. One common strategy is to scale width <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> linearly with fixed aspect ratio <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L/N</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Noci et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dey et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, however existing theory cannot justify such a practice as compute optimal or distinguish relative performance gain from width and depth from total parameter count. This leads us to our first open question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="95.21" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 95.21" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,95.21) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 89.3 C 0 92.57 2.64 95.21 5.91 95.21 L 544.09 95.21 C 547.36 95.21 550 92.57 550 89.3 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 71.1 L 548.03 71.1 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 79.69)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Q1: What sets optimal Transformer shapes and scaling laws?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:3.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="45.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.82)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How should width and depth scale under a compute budget in a transformer? Do transformer scaling laws only depend on total parameters or have different width and depth dependence?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we explore this question for in-context learning (ICL) problems, specifically in-context linear regression problems. In-context learning refers to the ability of models to condition their outputs on the past sequence of inputs provided by the user <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">without updating model parameters</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. This contrasts with in-weight learning (IWL) where information about the dataset is encoded in the model parameters during pre-training. Whereas many prior theoretical works on neural scaling laws essentially analyze the role of width, pretraining data, or pretraining time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bahri et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maloney et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Simon et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paquette et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., a</a>)</cite>, our work explores how depth, width, context length, and pretraining time influence the quality of ICL in self-attention models. In our investigations, we find that the architectural requirements to perform ICL regression depend significantly on the statistics of the pretraining ICL tasks. To address the first question, we are forced to investigate:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="95.98" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 95.98" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,95.98) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 90.07 C 0 93.33 2.64 95.98 5.91 95.98 L 544.09 95.98 C 547.36 95.98 550 93.33 550 90.07 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 71.87 L 548.03 71.87 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 80.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Q2: What task properties influence ICL solution</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 17.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:3.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="46.28" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.82)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S1.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How does the statistical structure of ICL tasks provided during pretraining influence the nature of the learned solution? Do these influence the optimal width/depth ratios?</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these questions, we develop a solvable model of deep linear attention for three distinct ICL data covariance structures. Our concrete novel contributions and findings are as follows:</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify a new asymptotic scaling for in-context regression with linear attention where context length <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, number of masked points per context <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, and contexts per step <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> all scale <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linearly</span> with dimension <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. This reduces the amount of compute and total data (by a factor of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>) to converge than prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and speeds up simulations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce three distinct ICL data models of increasing generality. We study <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">isotropic</span> input data and task vectors (<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ISO</span>). We then proceed to analyze fixed and structured (<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FS</span>) covariances for data and task vectors. Lastly, we examine an ICL distribution where data covariances are randomly rotated and structured (<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RRS</span>) across contexts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show with our asymptotic theory, that depth <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> benefits the first two data settings (<span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ISO</span> + <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FS</span>) only if <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\equiv\text{context length}/\text{data dimension}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mtext>context length</mtext><mo>/</mo><mtext>data dimension</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\equiv\text{context length}/\text{data dimension}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. In the limit of <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, there is no benefit from depth for these ICL tasks. Further, the FS setting is brittle with respect to variations in changes to the data covariance at test time. However, for the <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RRS</span> setting, increasing depth is beneficial for any distribution and a non-trivial width &amp; depth scaling.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a model width <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> bottleneck to our ICL model and study the pretraining scaling law for a model trained for <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> steps on a width <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> linear attention model in the <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RRS</span> setting. For powerlaw data, the scaling law takes the form of a Chinchilla scaling law but with width and depth contributing separate terms</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(t,N,L,P)=c_{t}\ t^{-\beta_{t}}+c_{N}\ N^{-\beta_{N}}+c_{L}\ L^{-\beta_{L}}+c_{P}\ P^{-\beta_{P}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(t,N,L,P)=c_{t}\ t^{-\beta_{t}}+c_{N}\ N^{-\beta_{N}}+c_{L}\ L^{-\beta_{L}}+c_{P}\ P^{-\beta_{P}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From this scaling law, we consider compute optimal joint scaling of width and depth. Depending on the structure of the ICL covariates, we obtain different scalings of <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\sim N^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\sim N^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> depends on properties of the data.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Related Works</h3>

<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Infinite Neural Network Width and Depth Limits and Commonly Used Joint Scalings.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The empirical fact that larger networks tend to perform better on natural data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hestness et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffmann et al., 2022</a>)</cite> has led to development of scaling procedures to stably increase the size of a model to allow optimization. The mean-field or <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P scaling theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geiger et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mei et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chizat &amp; Bach, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang &amp; Hu, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon &amp; Pehlevan, 2022b</a>)</cite> allows one to scale up the width of a model in a way that admits a feature learning infinite limit. Further, this scaling protocol provides consistent optimal hyperparameters, while delivering monotonic improvements in performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. The same program has been carried out for deep residual models, such as transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dey et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, while these infinite width and infinite depth scaling limits have been established to exist and perform better than finite models, no theory currently captures the <span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relative gains in performance from scaling up width or depth at fixed compute.</span> Understanding compute optimal shapes could help guide architectural choices when training large transformer models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theories of Compute Optimal Neural Scaling Laws.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the empirical scaling law results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al. (2020)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffmann et al. (2022)</a></cite>, many theoretical works have examined the generalization theory for fully trained kernel methods under power law features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Spigler et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bahri et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maloney et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Atanasov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Atanasov et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Defilippis et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. More recently, several efforts have begun to incorporate SGD dynamics into these models to gain a notion of compute (and compute optimal tradeoffs between parameters and training time) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon &amp; Pehlevan, 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paquette et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kunstner &amp; Bach, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., a</a>)</cite>. In these works, the model is essentially one or two layers and the notion of finite model size is introduced with a random projection of the features to an <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional space. In this way, existing theories more closely resemble scaling laws for width rather than a comparison where depth and width serve different functions. Recent empirical works have pointed out the utility of increasing depth (or virtual depth through looping) for tasks requiring reasoning such as solving Sudoku puzzles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and solving math problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geiping et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill &amp; Sabharwal (2025)</a></cite> study regular language recognition where a clear computational advantage to scaling depth instead of width is established and experimentally verified.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Empirical Studies of ICL.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2020)</a></cite> demonstrated that large pretrained language models such as GPT-3 exhibit remarkable in-context learning capabilities for natural language tasks. Following this finding, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al. (2022)</a></cite> empirically investigated the ability of transformers to learn simple function classes such as linear regression, sparse regression, and two-layer neural networks. Various works have studied emergence of ICL beyond language tasks, focusing on what data structures (from dynamical systems to classification problems) can be learned in-context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chan et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">McCracken et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Several works have offered experimental evidence that ICL implements Bayesian inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wurgaft et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panwar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>. Further investigations questioned whether attention is even strictly necessary for ICL by studying the performance of MLPs on these tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong &amp; Pehlevan, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kratsios &amp; Furuya, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theoretical Studies of ICL.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by empirical studies of ICL, many works have theoretically investigated how ICL algorithms such as in-context gradient descent can be implemented in transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pathak et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahankali et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Some works have demonstrated flexibility of the transformer to adapt to changing statistics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vladymyrov et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or performing model selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Studies have pointed out the need for sufficient pretraining task diversity for ICL generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raventós et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> which was theoretically analyzed in an asymptotic scaling limit for a shallow linear attention model by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Extensions to structured covariances and distribution shifts revealed the importance of train-test task alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Letey et al. (2025)</a></cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gatmiry et al. (2024)</a></cite> investigate the need for a sufficient number of residual stream steps (either by increasing true depth or loops of attention layers) to solve ICL distributions with multiple condition numbers with larger condition numbers requiring more depth. While many of these works study a final construction of weights, recent theory has described the training dynamics of one layer linear multi-head attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Data, Architecture and Reduced Model</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Deep Linear Attention Architecture</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most general model we study is a depth <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, residual linear attention model <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> that maps inputs contexts to output predictions. The data are formed as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> input-output pairs <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{x}_{\mu},y_{\mu})\}_{\mu=1}^{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{x}_{\mu},y_{\mu})\}_{\mu=1}^{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> evaluation points <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{x}^{\star}_{\mu},*)\}_{\mu=P+1}^{P+K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>μ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{x}^{\star}_{\mu},*)\}_{\mu=P+1}^{P+K}</annotation></semantics></math> (which do not carry target outputs) into a data matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{D}=\begin{bmatrix}\bm{x}_{1}&amp;...&amp;\bm{x}_{P}&amp;\bm{x}_{P+1}&amp;...&amp;\bm{x}_{P+K}\\
y_{1}&amp;...&amp;y_{P}&amp;*&amp;...&amp;*\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>∗</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>∗</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{D}=\begin{bmatrix}\bm{x}_{1}&amp;...&amp;\bm{x}_{P}&amp;\bm{x}_{P+1}&amp;...&amp;\bm{x}_{P+K}\\
y_{1}&amp;...&amp;y_{P}&amp;*&amp;...&amp;*\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> indicates masked target values on the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> evaluation points which are provided as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> entries. The evaluation tokens <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\{P+1,...,P+K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\{P+1,...,P+K\}</annotation></semantics></math> prevented from updating the model with a positional masking matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mu\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mu\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Model Definition, Masking Mechanics, Reduced-Γ Model ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). The model <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed from</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{h}^{1}_{\mu}=\bm{W}_{x}\bm{x}_{\mu}+\bm{w}_{y}y_{\mu},\quad\quad\bm{h}^{\ell+1}_{\mu}=\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu}+\frac{1}{LP}\sum_{\nu=1}^{P}\left(\bm{k}^{\ell}_{\nu}\cdot\bm{q}^{\ell}_{\mu}\right)\bm{v}^{\ell}_{\nu}\ ,\ \ell\in[L]\ ,\ \mu\in[P+K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{h}^{1}_{\mu}=\bm{W}_{x}\bm{x}_{\mu}+\bm{w}_{y}y_{\mu},\quad\quad\bm{h}^{\ell+1}_{\mu}=\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu}+\frac{1}{LP}\sum_{\nu=1}^{P}\left(\bm{k}^{\ell}_{\nu}\cdot\bm{q}^{\ell}_{\mu}\right)\bm{v}^{\ell}_{\nu}\ ,\ \ell\in[L]\ ,\ \mu\in[P+K]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{q}^{\ell}_{\mu}=\bm{W}_{q}^{\ell}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu}\ ,\ \bm{k}^{\ell}_{\mu}=\bm{W}_{k}^{\ell}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu}\ ,\ \bm{v}^{\ell}_{\mu}=\bm{W}_{v}^{\ell}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu},\quad\quad f_{\mu}=\bm{w}_{o}\cdot\bm{h}^{L}_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>q</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{q}^{\ell}_{\mu}=\bm{W}_{q}^{\ell}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu}\ ,\ \bm{k}^{\ell}_{\mu}=\bm{W}_{k}^{\ell}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu}\ ,\ \bm{v}^{\ell}_{\mu}=\bm{W}_{v}^{\ell}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu},\quad\quad f_{\mu}=\bm{w}_{o}\cdot\bm{h}^{L}_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The loss function for context <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}({\bm{D}})=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{\mu=P+1}^{P+K}(f_{\mu}-y_{\mu})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}({\bm{D}})=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{\mu=P+1}^{P+K}(f_{\mu}-y_{\mu})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and the full population loss is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{D}}\mathcal{L}(\bm{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝑫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{D}}\mathcal{L}(\bm{D})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution of context matrices. We stress that the operation <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{q}_{\mu}\cdot\bm{k}_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{q}_{\mu}\cdot\bm{k}_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <span id="S2.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linear attention</span> rather than commonly used in soft-max attention. For the regression tasks we consider, this model is sufficient to solve the ICL task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and aids theoretical tractability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Recurrent Reduced-<math id="S2.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> Model</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior works on ICL in linear regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we examine the minimal (simplest) reparameterization of the above model which can solve this task where the residual stream encodes <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> information in a subspace orthogonal to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{y}=\bm{w}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{y}=\bm{w}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{x}^{\top}\bm{w}_{y}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{x}^{\top}\bm{w}_{y}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}_{v}\propto\bm{w}_{y}\bm{w}_{y}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}_{v}\propto\bm{w}_{y}\bm{w}_{y}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Further, instead of optimizing separate hidden weight matrices, we consider <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">looped / universal transformers</span> where each attention block is the identical <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}^{\ell}_{i}=\bm{W}^{\ell^{\prime}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}^{\ell}_{i}=\bm{W}^{\ell^{\prime}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{k,q,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{k,q,v\}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghani et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gatmiry et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, though we relax this assumption in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Gradient Flow with Untied Γ Matrices ‣ 5 More Realistic Self Attention Models ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Different Parameters for Each Layer ‣ Appendix G Enhancing Realism of the Model ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a>. The reduced model then defines the predictor <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\bm{x}_{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\bm{x}_{\star})</annotation></semantics></math> for test point <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\bm{x}}_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\bm{x}}_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of a single matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times D}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\equiv\left(\bm{w}_{o}^{\top}\bm{W}_{v}\bm{w}_{y}\right)\bm{W}_{x}^{\top}\bm{W}_{k}^{\top}\bm{W}_{q}\bm{W}_{x}\ ,\ f(\bm{x}_{\star})=\frac{1}{LP}\bm{x}_{\star}^{\top}\bm{\Gamma}\sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1}\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\bm{\Gamma}\right)^{\ell}\bm{X}^{\top}\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>o</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⋆</mo><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚪</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\equiv\left(\bm{w}_{o}^{\top}\bm{W}_{v}\bm{w}_{y}\right)\bm{W}_{x}^{\top}\bm{W}_{k}^{\top}\bm{W}_{q}\bm{W}_{x}\ ,\ f(\bm{x}_{\star})=\frac{1}{LP}\bm{x}_{\star}^{\top}\bm{\Gamma}\sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1}\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\bm{\Gamma}\right)^{\ell}\bm{X}^{\top}\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}=\frac{1}{P}\bm{X}\bm{X}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>P</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}=\frac{1}{P}\bm{X}\bm{X}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical covariance. Due to the simplicity of this model and loss landscape structure, we will first focus on the gradient dynamics when we directly perform gradient descent on the matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Gradient Flow for Full Linear Attention ‣ 5 More Realistic Self Attention Models ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we consider learning dynamics on the decoupled collection of parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{W}_{i}\}_{i\in\{k,q,v\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{W}_{i}\}_{i\in\{k,q,v\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Learning Curves in Reduced Linear Attention Model</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Isotropic Covariates and Tasks (<span id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ISO</span>)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To start our investigation, we begin by considering <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional isotropic data and isotropic task distribution. For each context <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{D}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{D}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and each data point <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in[P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in[P]</annotation></semantics></math>, the distribution of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{x}_{\mu,c}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,{\bm{I}}\right)\quad\quad\bm{\beta}_{c}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,{\bm{I}})\ ,\ y_{\mu c}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{D}}\bm{\beta}_{c}\cdot\bm{x}^{\mu}_{c}+\sigma\epsilon^{\mu}_{c}\ ,\ \epsilon^{\mu}_{c}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{x}_{\mu,c}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,{\bm{I}}\right)\quad\quad\bm{\beta}_{c}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,{\bm{I}})\ ,\ y_{\mu c}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{D}}\bm{\beta}_{c}\cdot\bm{x}^{\mu}_{c}+\sigma\epsilon^{\mu}_{c}\ ,\ \epsilon^{\mu}_{c}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Shallow SGD Theory ‣ Appendix C Isotropic (ISO) Setting Theory ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>, we analyze SGD with batch of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> contexts sampled at each step in the proportional asymptotics <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,K,B,D\to\infty\ ,\ P/D=\alpha\ ,\ K/D=\kappa\ ,\ B/D=\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,K,B,D\to\infty\ ,\ P/D=\alpha\ ,\ K/D=\kappa\ ,\ B/D=\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, establishing that successful pretraining requires a total of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Bt=\Theta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Bt=\Theta(D)</annotation></semantics></math> contexts, each of size <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\Theta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\Theta(D)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/train_dynamics_vary_alpha.svg" id="S3.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/134;" width="176" height="134"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span><math id="S3.F1.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=16</annotation></semantics></math>, Linear Transformer</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/train_dynamics_vary_L.svg" id="S3.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/134;" width="176" height="134"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span><math id="S3.F1.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>, Linear Transformer</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/depth_vs_alpha_isotropic.svg" id="S3.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/135;" width="176" height="135"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Final Loss of The Transformer</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Deep linear self attention models trained with SGD on the ICL task with <span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isotropic covariates</span> with <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=32</annotation></semantics></math>. (a) Training dynamics for varying <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. (b) Increasing depth <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> can improve ICL predictions, especially for <math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>. (c) The final loss is well predicted by the theory of <math id="S3.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> steps of gradient descent with optimal learning rate for each <math id="S3.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,L)</annotation></semantics></math> pair. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="182.81" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 182.81" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,182.81) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 176.91 C 0 180.17 2.64 182.81 5.91 182.81 L 544.09 182.81 C 547.36 182.81 550 180.17 550 176.91 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 157.16 L 548.03 157.16 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 166.53)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Result 1: Gradient Flow for Isotropic Covariates (ISO)</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 19.51)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:9.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.41em;font-size:10pt;" height="131.57" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 125.84)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In the gradient flow limit, when training from initial condition <math id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚪</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, ICL pretraining on isotropic data at any depth <math id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≥</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to tracking a scaled identity matrix</span></span>
<span id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S3.E6"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\Gamma}(t)=\gamma(t)\bm{I}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\Gamma}(t)=\gamma(t)\bm{I}.</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Consequently, the gradient flow can be expressed as an evolution of the scalar <math id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S3.E7"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{tr}\left&lt;\bm{\hat{\Sigma}}\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\gamma(t)\bm{\hat{\Sigma}}\right)^{2L-1}\right&gt;=\int d\lambda\rho(\lambda)\lambda\left[1-L^{-1}\gamma(t)\lambda\right]^{2L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∫</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{tr}\left&lt;\bm{\hat{\Sigma}}\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\gamma(t)\bm{\hat{\Sigma}}\right)^{2L-1}\right&gt;=\int d\lambda\rho(\lambda)\lambda\left[1-L^{-1}\gamma(t)\lambda\right]^{2L-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where the average is over the empirical covariance <math id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\hat{\Sigma}}=\frac{1}{P}\bm{X}\bm{X}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑿</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\hat{\Sigma}}=\frac{1}{P}\bm{X}\bm{X}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> with Marchenko-Pastur eigenvalue distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. The final loss can be interpreted as the MSE loss for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> steps of GD with optimal step size</span></span>
<span id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S3.E8"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\star}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⋆</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\star}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\min_{\gamma}\ \text{tr}\left&lt;\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\gamma\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\right)^{2L}\right&gt;=\min_{\gamma}\int\rho(\lambda)\left(1-L^{-1}\gamma\lambda\right)^{2L}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">min</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">tr</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⟨</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∫</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\min_{\gamma}\ \text{tr}\left&lt;\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\gamma\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\right)^{2L}\right&gt;=\min_{\gamma}\int\rho(\lambda)\left(1-L^{-1}\gamma\lambda\right)^{2L}.</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss saturates to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\star}=(1+\alpha)^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\star}=(1+\alpha)^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> while for <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\star}=[1-\alpha]_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\star}=[1-\alpha]_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, which illustrates the gap in performance between a shallow model and a large depth model. We illustrate loss curves of varying depth <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> compared to pretrained linear transformers (dots) in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Isotropic Covariates and Tasks (ISO) ‣ 3 Learning Curves in Reduced Linear Attention Model ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (c). We extend to the case with label noise <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Gradient Flow in Deep Models ‣ Appendix C Isotropic (ISO) Setting Theory ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>. In this case optimal <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is smaller since early stopping acts as an effective regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Advani et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sonthalia et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/loss_landscape_gamma_alpha_0.5.svg" id="S3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/135;" width="176" height="135"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Loss Landscape <math id="S3.F2.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/loss_landscape_gamma_alpha_2.0.svg" id="S3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/135;" width="176" height="135"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Loss Landscape <math id="S3.F2.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=2.0</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/loss_landscape_gamma_vary_sigma.svg" id="S3.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/135;" width="176" height="135"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Effect of Noise <math id="S3.F2.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=16</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The loss landscape for the reduced <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> model with <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}=\gamma\bm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}=\gamma\bm{I}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the gradient flow limit. This limit is equivalent to <span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">optimal step size</span> selection for in-context GD. (a)-(b) The effect of depth <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and context length <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> on the loss. (c) Larger noise <math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> decreases the optimal <math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="93.29" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 93.29" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,93.29) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 87.38 C 0 90.64 2.64 93.29 5.91 93.29 L 544.09 93.29 C 547.36 93.29 550 90.64 550 87.38 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 69.18 L 548.03 69.18 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 77.77)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Result 2: Depth is Unnecessary for Long Contexts on Isotropic Covariates</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 13.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:3.15em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="43.59" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 43.59)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">If <math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=P/D\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=P/D\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the minimal ICL loss (among any depth <math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> models). Any larger depth <math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≥</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the same (zero if <math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>) loss in the <math id="S3.SS1.p4.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> limit.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Fixed Structured Covariance (<span id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FS</span>) <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> Preconditioned Gradient Descent</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we let the population covariance be arbitrary <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left&lt;\bm{x}\bm{x}^{\top}\right&gt;=\bm{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝚺</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left&lt;\bm{x}\bm{x}^{\top}\right&gt;=\bm{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> across all contexts and task correlations be given by the matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left&lt;\bm{\beta}\bm{\beta}^{\top}\right&gt;=\bm{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝜷</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left&lt;\bm{\beta}\bm{\beta}^{\top}\right&gt;=\bm{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. The ICL population loss takes the form</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\text{tr}\ \bm{\Omega}\left&lt;\left[\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\bm{\Gamma}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\right)^{L}\right]^{\top}\bm{\Sigma}\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\bm{\Gamma}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\right)^{L}\right&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>tr</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\text{tr}\ \bm{\Omega}\left&lt;\left[\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\bm{\Gamma}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\right)^{L}\right]^{\top}\bm{\Sigma}\left(\bm{I}-L^{-1}\bm{\Gamma}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\right)^{L}\right&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="95.21" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 95.21" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,95.21) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 89.3 C 0 92.57 2.64 95.21 5.91 95.21 L 544.09 95.21 C 547.36 95.21 550 92.57 550 89.3 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 71.1 L 548.03 71.1 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 79.69)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Result 3: Depth Unnecessary for Long Contexts with Fixed Covariance <math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathcolor="#FFFFFF">𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:3.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="45.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.82)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">When the ICL distribution involves fixed covariance across contexts, there is no benefit to increasing depth <math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> beyond <math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1</annotation></semantics></math> in the large context <math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> limit. For any <math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≥</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, zero ICL loss can be achieved in the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> limit by setting <math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}=L\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚪</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}=L\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We support this finding in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Fixed Structured Covariance (FS) → Preconditioned Gradient Descent ‣ 3 Learning Curves in Reduced Linear Attention Model ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (b) where we show that small depth models are not outperformed by deeper models even after very long training horizons. When the ICL pretraining distribution for <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, the model will memorize statistical information about the covariance of the inputs from the pretraining distribution. By preconditioning with the inverse of the data covariance <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model is capable of achieving zero loss after even a single step of in-context GD. The gradient flow dynamics of the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> matrix can be decomposed further in the case where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> commute.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/eig_evolution_fixed_covariance.svg" id="S3.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/131;" width="176" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Eigenvalue Evolution</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/losses_fixed_covariance.svg" id="S3.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/131;" width="176" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Depth unnecessary</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/ood_loss_fixed_covariance.svg" id="S3.F3.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/133;" width="176" height="133"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Brittleness to Distribution Shift</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Pretraining on <span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FS</span> ICL covariates leads to a solution that does not require depth but is brittle to distribution shift. (a) Evolution of the eigenvalues <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{k}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{k}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> matrix for depth <math id="S3.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=4</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of pretraining time <math id="S3.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> compared with infinite depth <math id="S3.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> theory (dashed black). (b) For powerlaw covariates, all depth models converge as a power law in <math id="S3.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. There is no asymptotic benefit to increasing depth beyond <math id="S3.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1</annotation></semantics></math>. (c) The ICL solution obtained when training from fixed covariance is <span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">brittle</span> to changes in the covariance <math id="S3.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}\to\exp(-\theta\bm{S})\bm{\Sigma}\exp(\theta\bm{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑺</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑺</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}\to\exp(-\theta\bm{S})\bm{\Sigma}\exp(\theta\bm{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="199.13" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 199.13" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,199.13) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 193.22 C 0 196.48 2.64 199.13 5.91 199.13 L 544.09 199.13 C 547.36 199.13 550 196.48 550 193.22 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 175.02 L 548.03 175.02 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 183.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Result 4: Decoupled Eigenvalue Dynamics During Pretraining on Fixed Covariances</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 17.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:10.55em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="149.43" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 145.97)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Suppose that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> are codiagonalizable with respective eigenvalues <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\omega_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\omega_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, when training from zero initialization, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is diagonal in the same basis with eigenvalues <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{k}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{k}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> that obey the following dynamics (see Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Fixed and Structured Covariance (FS) Setting ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>)</span></span>
<span id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S3.E10"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\gamma_{k}(t)=\omega_{k}\lambda_{k}^{2}\left(1-L^{-1}\lambda_{k}\gamma_{k}(t)\right)^{2L-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\gamma_{k}(t)=\omega_{k}\lambda_{k}^{2}\left(1-L^{-1}\lambda_{k}\gamma_{k}(t)\right)^{2L-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In the large depth limit <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> these dynamics have solution <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{k}(t)=\frac{1}{2\lambda_{k}}\ln(1+4\omega_{k}\lambda_{k}^{3}t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{k}(t)=\frac{1}{2\lambda_{k}}\ln(1+4\omega_{k}\lambda_{k}^{3}t)</annotation></semantics></math> generating the loss dynamics <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{L\to\infty}\mathcal{L}(t,L)=\sum_{k}\frac{\omega_{k}\lambda_{k}}{1+4\omega_{k}\lambda_{k}^{3}\ t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">lim</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{L\to\infty}\mathcal{L}(t,L)=\sum_{k}\frac{\omega_{k}\lambda_{k}}{1+4\omega_{k}\lambda_{k}^{3}\ t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under powerlaw source/capacity conditions where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}\sim k^{-\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}\sim k^{-\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\ell&gt;k}\lambda_{\ell}\omega_{\ell}\sim k^{-\nu\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\ell&gt;k}\lambda_{\ell}\omega_{\ell}\sim k^{-\nu\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the ICL loss scales as a powerlaw in pretraining time <math id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(t)\sim t^{-\frac{\beta}{\nu+\nu\beta+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(t)\sim t^{-\frac{\beta}{\nu+\nu\beta+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While pretraining the linear transformer in this setting can achieve zero loss for long contexts <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> even in shallow networks, the solution the model finds from gradient descent is <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">brittle</span> to properties of the pretraining data. Rather than learning a generic algorithm that solves ICL regression for any covariance <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, its solution is specialized to the pretraining covariance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="98.55" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 98.55" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,98.55) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 92.64 C 0 95.9 2.64 98.55 5.91 98.55 L 544.09 98.55 C 547.36 98.55 550 95.9 550 92.64 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 72.9 L 548.03 72.9 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 82.26)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Result 5: Brittleness of Fully Trained Model to Distribution Shift (OOD Loss)</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 18.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:3.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.32em;font-size:10pt;" height="47.31" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.82)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A depth <math id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> model is pretrained on ICL tasks with fixed covariances <math id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> for inputs and task vectors, but evaluated on new covariances <math id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime},\bm{\Omega}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime},\bm{\Omega}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The out-of-distribution loss is</span></span>
<span id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S3.E11"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{OOD}}=\text{tr}\ \bm{\Omega}^{\prime}\left[\left(\bm{I}-\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}\right)^{L}\right]^{\top}\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}\left(\bm{I}-\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}\right)^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">OOD</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">tr</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{OOD}}=\text{tr}\ \bm{\Omega}^{\prime}\left[\left(\bm{I}-\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}\right)^{L}\right]^{\top}\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}\left(\bm{I}-\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}\right)^{L}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We illustrate this brittleness in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Fixed Structured Covariance (FS) → Preconditioned Gradient Descent ‣ 3 Learning Curves in Reduced Linear Attention Model ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (c) where we define a family of new covariance matrices <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}=\exp(\theta\bm{S})\bm{\Sigma}\exp(-\theta\bm{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑺</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑺</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}=\exp(\theta\bm{S})\bm{\Sigma}\exp(-\theta\bm{S})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random skew-symmetric matrix. As <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> increases and <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes more dissimilar to <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, the OOD loss increases for all depths <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Random Rotated and Structured Covariances <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\implies" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\implies</annotation></semantics></math> In-Context GD</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we attempt to enhance the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">covariance diversity</span> across contexts. To do so, we now allow that each context <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> has a random data and task covariances which are randomly rotated</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{x}^{\mu}_{c}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,\bm{\Sigma}_{c}\right)\ ,\ \bm{\Sigma}_{c}=\bm{O}_{c}\bm{\Lambda}\bm{O}_{c}^{\top}\ ,\ \bm{\Omega}_{c}=\bm{O}_{c}\bm{\Omega}\bm{O}_{c}^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑶</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑶</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑶</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑶</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{x}^{\mu}_{c}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,\bm{\Sigma}_{c}\right)\ ,\ \bm{\Sigma}_{c}=\bm{O}_{c}\bm{\Lambda}\bm{O}_{c}^{\top}\ ,\ \bm{\Omega}_{c}=\bm{O}_{c}\bm{\Omega}\bm{O}_{c}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{O}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑶</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{O}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal matrix sampled from the Haar measure. The idea to pretrain with a diverse set of covariances <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> across contexts <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is to encourage the model to learn a <span id="S3.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generic</span> in-context learning algorithm that is not specifically tailored to a particular data covariance <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. By introducing the random rotation across contexts, the model cannot encode a whitening transform of the data in the matrix <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> which prevents a zero loss solution in a shallow model with depth <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, even the <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P/D\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P/D\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> limit has the potential to exhibit a nontrivial scaling law in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="80.35" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 80.35" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,80.35) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 74.45 C 0 77.71 2.64 80.35 5.91 80.35 L 544.09 80.35 C 547.36 80.35 550 77.71 550 74.45 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 56.24 L 548.03 56.24 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 64.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Result 6: Gradient Flow Generates Isotropic <math id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathcolor="#FFFFFF">𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 18.22)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:1.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.32em;font-size:10pt;" height="30.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.21)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Gradient flow on the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>-reduced model maintains the isotropy condition <math id="S3.SS3.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}(t)=\gamma(t)\bm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}(t)=\gamma(t)\bm{I}</annotation></semantics></math> with</span></span>
<span id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S3.E13"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\gamma(t)=\text{tr}\bm{\Lambda}^{2}\bm{\Omega}\left(1-L^{-1}\gamma(t)\bm{\Lambda}\right)^{2L-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚲</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚲</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\gamma(t)=\text{tr}\bm{\Lambda}^{2}\bm{\Omega}\left(1-L^{-1}\gamma(t)\bm{\Lambda}\right)^{2L-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This indicates that, provided the covariance is randomly rotated across contexts, the behavior of the learned solution is unconditioned in-context GD (see Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Randomly Rotated and Structured Covariance (RRS) Setting ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>). In the next section, we explore the consequences of this finding for optimal shapes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Model of Compute Optimal Neural Scaling Laws</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the third setting with power law features and also introduce a notion of width through a projection matrix <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Rather than training on <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional inputs <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model has access to <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional features <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{x}}=\bm{A}\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{x}}=\bm{A}\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, which leads to the following condition of <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\bm{x}}=\bm{A}\bm{x}\quad{\implies}\quad\bm{\Gamma}(t)=\gamma(t)\left(\bm{A}\bm{A}^{\top}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\bm{x}}=\bm{A}\bm{x}\quad{\implies}\quad\bm{\Gamma}(t)=\gamma(t)\left(\bm{A}\bm{A}^{\top}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As before, the loss can again be viewed as a function of the scale parameter <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. We provide a recipe to compute it below by computing the average over the random orthgonal matrix, resulting in a two-point deterministic equivalent for free products<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          <span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Two-point</span> refers to the correlation function of two resolvents evaluated at different arguments, rather than the one point function which is a single resolvent and determines only the spectrum of the random matrix.</span></span></span>, in the same spirit of the results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Atanasov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, using a saddle point method (Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Two Point Deterministic Equivalent for General Free Product From Dynamical Mean Field Theory ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="196.8" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 196.8" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,196.8) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 190.9 C 0 194.16 2.64 196.8 5.91 196.8 L 544.09 196.8 C 547.36 196.8 550 194.16 550 190.9 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 155.32 L 548.03 155.32 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 163.91)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:1.95em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="29.67" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.98)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Result 7: Two-Point Deterministic Equivalent (DMFT Correlation) for the Loss Landscape</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 17.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:9.13em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="129.73" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 126.27)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The loss function <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\left&lt;|\bm{\Lambda}^{1/2}[\bm{I}-\gamma(t)L^{-1}\bm{O}\left(\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}\right)^{2}\bm{O}^{\top}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}]^{L}\bar{\bm{\beta}}|^{2}\right&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⟨</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚲</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑶</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝜷</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\left&lt;|\bm{\Lambda}^{1/2}[\bm{I}-\gamma(t)L^{-1}\bm{O}\left(\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}\right)^{2}\bm{O}^{\top}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}]^{L}\bar{\bm{\beta}}|^{2}\right&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> can be explicitly averaged over the random orthogonal matrix <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{O}</annotation></semantics></math> and expressed as a deterministic function</span></span>
<span id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S4.Ex2"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(t,N,L,P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(t,N,L,P)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int\frac{d\omega d\omega^{\prime}}{(2\pi)^{2}}\left(1+L^{-1}\gamma(t)i\omega\right)^{L}\left(1+L^{-1}\gamma(t)i\omega^{\prime}\right)^{L}\mathcal{C}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∫</mo><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int\frac{d\omega d\omega^{\prime}}{(2\pi)^{2}}\left(1+L^{-1}\gamma(t)i\omega\right)^{L}\left(1+L^{-1}\gamma(t)i\omega^{\prime}\right)^{L}\mathcal{C}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.E15"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{C}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{C}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{tr}\ \bm{\Lambda}\left[i\omega+\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)\bm{\Lambda}\right]^{-1}\left[\bm{\Omega}-\Psi_{\chi\chi}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})\right]\left[i\omega^{\prime}+\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega^{\prime})\bm{\Lambda}\right]^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">tr</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">χ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚲</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">χ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">χ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚲</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{tr}\ \bm{\Lambda}\left[i\omega+\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)\bm{\Lambda}\right]^{-1}\left[\bm{\Omega}-\Psi_{\chi\chi}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})\right]\left[i\omega^{\prime}+\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega^{\prime})\bm{\Lambda}\right]^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">χ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\chi\chi}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">χ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\chi\chi}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are deterministic functions that depend on the spectra of <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}</annotation></semantics></math> (Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Two Point Deterministic Equivalent for General Free Product From Dynamical Mean Field Theory ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). For example to obtain <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">χ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> we first solve</span></span>
<span id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S4.E16"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i\omega_{(\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A})^{2}}(\tau)i\omega_{\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}}(\tau)=\frac{\tau-1}{\tau}i\omega\implies\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)=\frac{i\omega}{i\omega_{(\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A})^{2}}(\tau)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚺</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">χ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i\omega_{(\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A})^{2}}(\tau)i\omega_{\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}}(\tau)=\frac{\tau-1}{\tau}i\omega\implies\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)=\frac{i\omega}{i\omega_{(\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A})^{2}}(\tau)}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S4.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> for matrix <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S4.p2.pic1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\text{tr}\bm{M}\left(\bm{M}+i\omega_{M}\right)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑴</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\text{tr}\bm{M}\left(\bm{M}+i\omega_{M}\right)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/losses_vary_beta.svg" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/131;" width="176" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Pretraining time <math id="S4.F4.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> scaling</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/loss_landscape_powerlaw.svg" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/131;" width="176" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Loss landscape vs Depth</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/losses_gamma_model_vary_L.svg" id="S4.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/131;" width="176" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Learning Curves Varying <math id="S4.F4.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Loss dynamics for powerlaw data. (a) Varying the source exponent <math id="S4.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that the scaling with pretraining time has exponent <math id="S4.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\beta}{2+\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\beta}{2+\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. (b) The loss landscape across depths <math id="S4.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> for the scalar <math id="S4.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> parameter exhibits minima at <math id="S4.F4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\approx L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\approx L</annotation></semantics></math>. (c) The training dynamics of the reduced-<math id="S4.F4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> model exhibit <math id="S4.F4.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t^{-\beta/(2+\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{-\beta/(2+\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math> decay before hitting an asymptote which scales as <math id="S4.F4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{-\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{-\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Orthogonal projection and A is Structured Wishart ‣ Appendix F Scaling Law Theory ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a> we provide formulas for <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{v\chi}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{vv}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{vv}(\omega,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in the case that <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is a rank <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> projection (has <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues equal to <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}=\frac{1}{P}\bm{X}\bm{X}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>P</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}=\frac{1}{P}\bm{X}\bm{X}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is a structured Wishart matrix. This formula provides the full asymptotics. We can further extract Chinchilla scaling laws for powerlaw data. We demonstrate the need for both width and depth in Figures <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4 Model of Compute Optimal Neural Scaling Laws ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Model of Compute Optimal Neural Scaling Laws ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="210.43" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 210.43" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,210.43) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 204.52 C 0 207.79 2.64 210.43 5.91 210.43 L 544.09 210.43 C 547.36 210.43 550 207.79 550 204.52 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 186.32 L 548.03 186.32 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 194.92)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Result 8: Scaling Law for Power Law Features and Optimal Shapes</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:11.42em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="160.73" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 158.04)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Assume source and capacity conditions for the eigenvalues and target coefficients <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}\equiv\Omega_{kk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}\equiv\Omega_{kk}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S4.E17"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{\ell&gt;k}\lambda_{\ell}\ \omega_{k}\sim k^{-\nu\beta}\ ,\ \lambda_{k}\sim k^{-\nu}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ω</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{\ell&gt;k}\lambda_{\ell}\ \omega_{k}\sim k^{-\nu\beta}\ ,\ \lambda_{k}\sim k^{-\nu},</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where source and capacity <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> control the complexity of each in-context linear regression problem. Let the matrix <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be rank <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and frozen. Then the loss follows a Chinchilla neural scaling law in the resources of time <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, width <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, depth <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, and context length <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S4.E18"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(t,N,L,P)\approx c_{t}\ t^{-\frac{\beta}{2+\beta}}+c_{N}\ N^{-\nu\beta}+c_{L}\ L^{-\beta}+c_{P}\ P^{-\nu\beta}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(t,N,L,P)\approx c_{t}\ t^{-\frac{\beta}{2+\beta}}+c_{N}\ N^{-\nu\beta}+c_{L}\ L^{-\beta}+c_{P}\ P^{-\nu\beta},</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S4.p4.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">in the sense that taking any three of the resources to infinity leaving the last fixed results in a powerlaw in the remaining resource, e.g. <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{N,L,P\to\infty}\mathcal{L}(t,N,L,P)=\Theta\left(t^{-\frac{\beta}{2+\beta}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">lim</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{N,L,P\to\infty}\mathcal{L}(t,N,L,P)=\Theta\left(t^{-\frac{\beta}{2+\beta}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. As a consequence, at fixed compute <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=tP^{2}N^{2}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=tP^{2}N^{2}L</annotation></semantics></math> the optimal width and depth scale as <math id="S4.p4.pic1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\propto N^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∝</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\propto N^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/compute_scaling_fixed_L_more_width.svg" id="S4.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/123;" width="176" height="123"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Fixed Depth <math id="S4.F5.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/compute_scaling_fixed_N_more_depth.svg" id="S4.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/125;" width="176" height="125"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Fixed Width <math id="S4.F5.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=8</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/compute_scaling_joint_linear_more_NL.svg" id="S4.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/131;" width="176" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Optimal Joint Scaling <math id="S4.F5.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\propto N^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\propto N^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Increasing width and depth alone is insufficient to obtain monotonic improvements on powerlaw data with random covariance across contexts. (a) Scaling only width leads to a depth bottleneck (dashed red line). (b) Scaling only depth leads to a width bottleneck (dashed red line). (c) Increasing <math id="S4.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously achieves monotonic improvement with compute. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>More Realistic Self Attention Models</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss more realistic models which exhibit similar training dynamics and depth dependence. First, we describe the dynamics when the <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝚪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices are not tied. Second, we provide a theory of training when each of the weight matrices is optimized separately. Lastly, we provide experiments with softmax attention models trained with Adam.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Gradient Flow with Untied <math id="S5.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> Matrices</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we consider the effect of untied <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝚪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices across layers <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[L]</annotation></semantics></math>. When each of the weights are optimized separately with a learning rate that is upscaled by depth <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\eta_{0}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\eta_{0}L</annotation></semantics></math>, the dynamics are actually equivalent to the recurrent model that we presented previously under the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RRS</span> noise-free setting. In this setting, the matrices remain isotropic <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}^{\ell}=\gamma^{\ell}\bm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝚪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}^{\ell}=\gamma^{\ell}\bm{I}</annotation></semantics></math> under gradient flow and leading to <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">permutation symmetric</span> loss function</p>
<table id="S5.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\{\gamma^{\ell}\})=\left&lt;\left|\bm{\Lambda}^{1/2}\prod_{\ell=1}^{L}\left[\bm{I}-L^{-1}\gamma^{\ell}\bm{O}\left(\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}\right)^{2}\bm{O}^{\top}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\right]\bar{\bm{\beta}}\right|^{2}\right&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚲</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑶</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\{\gamma^{\ell}\})=\left&lt;\left|\bm{\Lambda}^{1/2}\prod_{\ell=1}^{L}\left[\bm{I}-L^{-1}\gamma^{\ell}\bm{O}\left(\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}\right)^{2}\bm{O}^{\top}\hat{\bm{\Sigma}}\right]\bar{\bm{\beta}}\right|^{2}\right&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the average is over <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{O}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\bm{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\bm{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The evolution of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{\ell}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{\ell}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> under gradient flow will maintain balance <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{\ell}(t)=\gamma^{k}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{\ell}(t)=\gamma^{k}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> if they start in a balanced initial condition such as <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{\ell}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{\ell}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. As a consequence, the loss dynamics will be <span id="S5.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">identical to the recurrent model that we analyzed in previous sections</span>. We provide additional details in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Different Parameters for Each Layer ‣ Appendix G Enhancing Realism of the Model ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A7.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ G.1.1 Noise Free Loss Function at Large Width and Context Length ‣ G.1 Different Parameters for Each Layer ‣ Appendix G Enhancing Realism of the Model ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Gradient Flow for Full Linear Attention</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we consider gradient flow on all of the attention weights <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{W}_{k},\bm{W}_{q},\bm{W}_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{W}_{k},\bm{W}_{q},\bm{W}_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> separately rather than gradient flow on the <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> matrix. This corresponds to the dynamical system</p>
<table id="S5.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\bm{W}_{i}=-\frac{\partial}{\partial\bm{W}_{i}}\mathcal{L}\ ,\ i\in\{x,y,k,q,v,o\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\bm{W}_{i}=-\frac{\partial}{\partial\bm{W}_{i}}\mathcal{L}\ ,\ i\in\{x,y,k,q,v,o\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The dynamics of this model from small initialization are theoretically tractable as a set of low dimensional ODEs (see Appendix <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2 Decoupling Weights in Attention Layers ‣ Appendix G Enhancing Realism of the Model ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a>), but suffers some defects due to transient blowup and recovery in the scale of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, if we fix these weights to unit norm, the dynamics of the above model reduces to a <span id="S5.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">one-dimensional ODE</span> much like the reduced-<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="227.23" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 227.23" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,227.23) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3333B3;" fill="#3333B3" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 221.32 C 0 224.59 2.64 227.23 5.91 227.23 L 544.09 227.23 C 547.36 227.23 550 224.59 550 221.32 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="0.95"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 205.81 L 548.03 205.81 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 211.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Result 9: Gradient Flow on Full Linear Attention Module</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 18.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:12.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.32em;font-size:10pt;" height="180.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 175.73)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Consider pretraining a linear transformer on randomly rotated covariance ICL data distribution. Fix read-in weights <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒘</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and readout weights <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒘</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{y}=\bm{w}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒘</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒘</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{y}=\bm{w}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\bm{w}_{y}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒘</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\bm{w}_{y}|=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Initialize the other weights to be small <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}|\bm{W}_{x}|^{2}=|\bm{W}_{k}|^{2}=|\bm{W}_{q}|^{2}=|\bm{W}_{k}|^{2}=\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}|\bm{W}_{x}|^{2}=|\bm{W}_{k}|^{2}=|\bm{W}_{q}|^{2}=|\bm{W}_{k}|^{2}=\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≪</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The gradient dynamics will maintain a balanced condition where <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\bm{W}_{i}(t)|=|\bm{W}_{j}(t)|=w(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\bm{W}_{i}(t)|=|\bm{W}_{j}(t)|=w(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in\{x,k,q,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in\{x,k,q,v\}</annotation></semantics></math> where the scalar <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(t)</annotation></semantics></math> evolves as</span></span>
<span id="S5.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S5.E21"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}w(t)=w(t)^{4}\ \text{tr}\bm{\Lambda}^{2}\bm{\Omega}\left(\bm{I}-w(t)^{5}\bm{\Lambda}\right)^{2L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚲</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚲</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}w(t)=w(t)^{4}\ \text{tr}\bm{\Lambda}^{2}\bm{\Omega}\left(\bm{I}-w(t)^{5}\bm{\Lambda}\right)^{2L-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This can be interpreted as gradient flow on the loss function for the reduced <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> model under the reparameterization <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)\to w(t)^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)\to w(t)^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. For powerlaw covariates with source exponent <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, this gives a powerlaw scaling with pretraining time <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S5.SS2.p2.pic1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S5.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S5.E22"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(t,L)\sim c_{t}\ t^{-\frac{5\beta}{5\beta+2}}+c_{L}\ L^{-\beta}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(t,L)\sim c_{t}\ t^{-\frac{5\beta}{5\beta+2}}+c_{L}\ L^{-\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span>
<span rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We show the learning curves for this full attention module for isotropic covariates and powerlaw covariates in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.3 Nonlinear (Softmax) Attention ‣ 5 More Realistic Self Attention Models ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The theoretical dynamics and predicted powerlaw exponent for this new dynamical system closely match the predictions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Nonlinear (Softmax) Attention</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also provide experiments showing that similar scaling behavior holds in softmax attention models trained with Adam. In this setting, the attention block has the form <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}^{\ell+1}_{\mu}=\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu}+L^{-1}\sum_{\nu=1}^{P}A_{\mu\nu}\bm{W}_{v}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}^{\ell+1}_{\mu}=\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu}+L^{-1}\sum_{\nu=1}^{P}A_{\mu\nu}\bm{W}_{v}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mu\nu}^{\ell}=\frac{1}{Z_{\mu}}\exp(\bm{h}_{\nu}^{\top}\bm{W}_{k}\bm{W}_{q}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mu\nu}^{\ell}=\frac{1}{Z_{\mu}}\exp(\bm{h}_{\nu}^{\top}\bm{W}_{k}\bm{W}_{q}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mu}=\sum_{\nu}\exp(\bm{h}_{\nu}^{\top}\bm{W}_{k}\bm{W}_{q}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>ν</mi></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mu}=\sum_{\nu}\exp(\bm{h}_{\nu}^{\top}\bm{W}_{k}\bm{W}_{q}\bm{h}^{\ell}_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> as the normalization factor. We train with Adam and show depth is still beneficial to ICL performance in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.3 Nonlinear (Softmax) Attention ‣ 5 More Realistic Self Attention Models ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (c) and <a href="#A7.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ G.2 Decoupling Weights in Attention Layers ‣ Appendix G Enhancing Realism of the Model ‣ Theory of Scaling Laws for In-Context Regression: Depth, Width, Context and Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, consistent with the phenomenology of our linear attention model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/isotropic_theory_vs_expt.svg" id="S5.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/135;" width="176" height="135"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Loss Isotropic Features <math id="S5.F6.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/dim_free_theory_weight_decoupled_vs_expt.svg" id="S5.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/131;" width="176" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Powerlaw <math id="S5.F6.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.25</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F6.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01098v1/SOFTMAX_T20000_NO_UPSCALING_loss_vs_t.svg" id="S5.F6.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/131;" width="176" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Softmax Attention Model</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>(a)-(b) Dynamics of the full linear attention module with separate <math id="S5.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{W}_{x},\bm{W}_{q},\bm{W}_{k},\bm{W}_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{W}_{x},\bm{W}_{q},\bm{W}_{k},\bm{W}_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of depth <math id="S5.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> agree with a theory generated through reparameterization <math id="S5.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to w^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to w^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> as described in Result 9. (c) Softmax attention dynamics.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conclusion</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work we analyzed a solvable model of in-context learning with deep linear attention. We showed that the pretraining statistics strongly determine the type of solution the model converges to and under what conditions scaling depth is necessary at large context lengths. When training on fixed covariance structures, large depth is not necessary (at infinite context length) as the model learns a preconditioner that whitens the data. However, this learned solution is brittle to changes in the data covariance. When training on tasks where the data covariance is randomly rotated across contexts, the model learns a general purpose in-context gradient descent algorithm and exhibits a separable Chinchilla neural scaling law where limited width and depth can both bottleneck performance. Lastly, we show these results are robust to reparameterization of the attention blocks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations and Future Directions</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our work presents an advance in the solvable models of neural scaling laws and the structure of ICL in linear attention, there are many current limitations. The primary limitation is that we focus on linear regression tasks solved with linear attention. Characterization of more general ICL problems such as nonlinear function approximation and nonlinear attention could provide more insights into realistic in-context function approximation. Further, our analysis is focused on online learning in the present work. Future work could investigate overfitting effects caused by repeating tasks or context matrices during training, perhaps with dynamical mean field theory techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mignacco et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Montanari &amp; Urbani, 2025</a>)</cite>. Future work could also examine the role of large learning rate effects during pretraining dynamics. Another direction that could be interesting to explore in future works is scaling up the number of loops dynamically as the network trains to significantly reduce the total compute required.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
